WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.321 --> 00:00:10.510
[FOREIGN] That is a well-known Swahili

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:15.862
proverb that means unity is strength,

00:00:15.862 --> 00:00:19.114
division is weakness.

00:00:19.114 --> 00:00:22.108
At first glance I may not look like it,
but I'm from Kenya and

00:00:22.108 --> 00:00:25.020
I've always been a very proud Kenyan.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.900
I represented the country for over ten
years as an international swimmer.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:33.650
And was given the biggest honor of my life
when I was chosen to carry the Kenyan

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:38.289
flag into the opening ceremony of
the London 2012 Olympic Games.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.000
My swimming career took
me all over the world and

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.970
within that, many parts of Africa.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:52.330
I visited cities from Cape Town to
Casablanca, from Dakar to Dar es Salaam.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:55.519
And I always felt incredibly at
home in all of these cities.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.570
As I was travelling around Africa and
competing, I was fiercely competitive.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:05.670
And I wanted to do everything I could
to get my hand on the wall first and

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.130
bring a medal back home to Kenya.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.660
But at the same time, I formed deep

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:16.540
friendships with many of the swimmers I
was up against from just about every one

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:21.510
of the 55 nations recognized
by the African union.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:28.380
And I'm sure the implications of these
experiences were seeded back then and

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.620
begun to germinate.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:34.999
But it's only over the last year that I've
begun to understand their deeper meaning.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:40.969
I now believe that it will be better for
Kenya, Africa, and

00:01:40.969 --> 00:01:44.680
the world if Africa were
to become more united.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.410
The more united Africa will
mean a more prosperous Africa.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:54.610
And we'll finally complete the Africa
rising narrative that is being told

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:55.460
the world over.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:59.700
And in these times of
rising nationalism and

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.410
isolationism in different pockets
of the world, a more united Africa

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:08.570
could just be the inspiration the world
needs for greater global unity.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.680
When I arrived at Stanford in 2016, one of
the first things I did was interview for

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.090
position on the leadership team for

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:21.630
the 10th annual African Business
Conference held here on campus.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.500
I was part of the group of students
from all over Africa, not only that,

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.080
all over the world.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.020
And we worked hard to
try to put on the most

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.760
successful African Business Conference
we'd ever had here in GSB.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:39.360
Pulled a lot of all nighters and we
ended up putting on one of the most well

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.578
attended student organized conferences
in the history of the GSB.

00:02:43.578 --> 00:02:44.760
I was very proud.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.310
We were all very proud
about what we had achieved.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:53.320
And as I thought about it, again the
implications, they didn't really hit me.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.735
I'm at Stanford, of course I'm going to be
in part of a high performing team, right?

00:02:56.735 --> 00:02:58.574
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:58.574 --> 00:03:00.956
&gt;&gt; So conference experience done,

00:03:00.956 --> 00:03:02.320
went back to work.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.860
Out of that experience at the conference
though, I started an interview channel.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:11.160
And sat down with some of my classmates,
run some interviews.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.660
And many of them are actually
in the audience today.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.840
And out of that experience I
got invited to go and cover

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:21.600
the silicon valley African film festival
that is held every year here in San Jose.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:27.960
At the festival, I was part of a group
of people, again from all over Africa,

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.290
from all over the world in
fact united in celebrating

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.450
the unique cultures of different
countries of the continent.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:38.040
But it really felt that we're all coming
together with one voice in celebration.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.730
And as I was running around the conference
trying to source interviews for

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:50.360
the red carpet, I bumped into this
one man called Ayouba Karamoko.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:53.490
As I started questioning him, I started
to learn a little bit about him and

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:57.400
that he's a musician,
martial artist, teacher, and

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.130
I'm asking where he's from and
he says, I'm from Africa.

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:04.581
I said, yeah, most people here are,
can you be a little more specific?

00:04:04.581 --> 00:04:06.191
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:06.191 --> 00:04:09.461
&gt;&gt; So he grudgingly tells me he's from

00:04:09.461 --> 00:04:11.098
the Ivory Coast.

00:04:11.098 --> 00:04:14.442
And we continue interview and
I'm thinking, okay,

00:04:14.442 --> 00:04:17.800
I'm not sure why he's being so
grudging about this.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.050
And I asked him what brings
him to the film festival?

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:28.270
And he talks about coming to support the
African filmmakers that were there, but

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.680
also that he's releasing a new album.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.116
The name of the album,
The United States of Africa,

00:04:35.116 --> 00:04:39.370
Act 1, the awakening.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:42.130
And looking back that spoke to me
a lot of what was going on for

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:46.030
me, but I was still resistant,
I was like big idea, yes.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:48.400
But realistic, not so sure.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.860
Came back to Standford got back to work.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:58.154
And then I heard that one of my
heroes was coming to Silicon Valley.

00:04:58.154 --> 00:05:02.003
And not just to Silicon Valley,
he'd be coming right here to the GSB to

00:05:02.003 --> 00:05:04.825
speak on the very stage
I'm standing on right now.

00:05:04.825 --> 00:05:07.910
His name is Strive Masiyiwa.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:11.550
For those who don't know,
he's one of Africa's great businessmen.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:16.852
He's built a telecom business that spans
the whole continent connecting people,

00:05:16.852 --> 00:05:19.284
and he started it with just $500.

00:05:19.284 --> 00:05:22.471
He was persecuted under Mugabe's
regime in Zimbabwe, and

00:05:22.471 --> 00:05:25.670
against all odds,
has built something quite incredible.

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:30.770
And I was part of 20 students that
were actually invited to come and

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:32.470
have lunch with him after he spoke.

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:39.300
And so we all assembled in Sewell
Boardroom in Bass Library to wait for

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:40.600
him to enter the room.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:45.420
And as we're lining up,
he enters the room and I put out my

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:50.250
hand to greet him as one always does.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:54.070
And I instinctively say,
sir, it's great to meet you.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.859
My name is Jason, I'm from Kenya.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:05:59.590
And he doesn't say anything.

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:03.650
So we're there, holding hands,
I'm clasping his.

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:06.810
And he turns to the rest
of the students assembled.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.460
And he says,
I want you to introduce yourself, but

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:12.370
I want you to say you're from Africa.

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:15.250
And then tell me your town or
city of origin.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:17.970
So, I try again.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.460
My name is Jason.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:23.846
I'm from Africa, in the city of Nairobi.

00:06:23.846 --> 00:06:27.380
And now the synopsis in my head
were firing out of control,

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:30.626
because I do has been a little of,
I'd say offended,

00:06:30.626 --> 00:06:34.688
when people abroad would refer to
Africa as if it were one country.

00:06:34.688 --> 00:06:39.394
And he was Strive Masiyiwa,
one of my heroesm telling me not to

00:06:39.394 --> 00:06:43.933
vilify that kind of thinking,
but to in fact encourage it.

00:06:43.933 --> 00:06:46.996
And the challenge he laid
out to us as future business

00:06:46.996 --> 00:06:48.743
leaders in Africa was clear.

00:06:48.743 --> 00:06:52.420
In order to drive a more
progressive development agenda,

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.378
we have to become more united.

00:06:54.378 --> 00:06:58.748
As a complete market,
Africa is 1.2 billion people,

00:06:58.748 --> 00:07:01.755
$4.4 trillion combined economy,

00:07:01.755 --> 00:07:07.413
youngest population in the world
that is expected to double by 2050.

00:07:07.413 --> 00:07:11.126
He said this population growth
could be a blessing or a curse and

00:07:11.126 --> 00:07:14.690
it's incumbent on us to
make sure it's a blessing.

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:16.760
To do that we need to become more united.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:19.530
Some more goods are used internally.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:23.953
Increased trade between the thriving
cities that I've been so

00:07:23.953 --> 00:07:29.126
lucky to visit and we have to form teams,
like I was a part of at the business

00:07:29.126 --> 00:07:35.067
conference to lead organizations that can
provide the jobs that we'll be needing.

00:07:35.067 --> 00:07:38.340
And not to say there's not going to
be a lot of challenges along the way.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.900
This is a difficult proposition.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:43.380
But it doesn't mean it's not
something we should strive for.

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:45.430
I mean,

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:50.375
it's oftentimes that the things most worth
doing in life are the most difficult.

00:07:54.295 --> 00:07:58.945
Since I last gave this talk,
six weeks ago, there have been

00:07:58.945 --> 00:08:03.879
some developments and
44 African nations have now signed out

00:08:03.879 --> 00:08:09.020
an agreement to create
the African Continental Free Trade Area.

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:12.034
It's a first step and
the momentum is there,

00:08:12.034 --> 00:08:16.887
but we must continue to accelerate it
to get to a United States of Africa.

00:08:19.195 --> 00:08:25.874
[FOREIGN] It is easier to cross the ocean,

00:08:25.874 --> 00:08:31.577
if we're all swimming together.

00:08:31.577 --> 00:08:35.175
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:35.175 --> 00:08:38.841
[MUSIC]

